    <section class="lf-article-section">
      <h2>Month-by-month Christmas lighting marketing plan</h2>
      <p>Each month has a different job. The aim is to connect
      the marketing calendar with actual installation operations.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="lf-article-section"><h2>January — Review results and retain customers</h2><p>Review the previous season&#x27;s verified inquiries, qualified quotes, bookings and revenue by channel where data exists. Record which neighborhoods, project types and installation windows were most practical. Confirm takedown and service follow-through. Ask satisfied customers for genuine reviews, with no fabricated claims or incentives that violate platform rules. Clean CRM records and document lessons before next season&#x27;s campaigns.</p></section>
<section class="lf-article-section"><h2>February — Build the customer retention plan</h2><p>Segment previous customers by residential, commercial, repeat service and project scope. Identify who may want to renew or expand a display. Review permission to contact customers and prepare helpful early planning messages. Photograph completed projects only where you have the right to use that imagery.</p></section>
<section class="lf-article-section"><h2>March — Audit the website and quote funnel</h2><p>Check mobile usability, form submissions, conversion tracking, calendar routing, CRM ownership and contact preferences. Update service-area coverage, FAQs and installation offerings. Remove outdated pricing and capacity claims. Make sure the website explains design, installation, maintenance and takedown services accurately.</p></section>
<section class="lf-article-section"><h2>April — Prepare creative and local search visibility</h2><p>Organize the best authorized before-and-after photographs and installation footage. Prepare image ads, vertical video and short Reels showing realistic results. Check relevant Google Business Profile information and local service pages. Avoid mass-producing nearly identical city pages or inventing customer testimonials.</p></section>
<section class="lf-article-section"><h2>May — Reach out to commercial prospects</h2><p>Commercial properties, hotels, retail locations and HOAs can have longer planning and approval cycles. Prepare an offer that explains design options, consultation requirements and the quotation process. Begin targeted outreach where suitable, rather than assuming every commercial buyer books at the same time.</p></section>
<section class="lf-article-section"><h2>June — Prepare the campaigns and qualification rules</h2><p>Research installation-intent keyword themes, build negative keyword candidates and draft search ad copy. Produce several Meta creative angles, including convenience, transformation and early planning. Configure location, project type, budget and preferred quote method in the funnel according to the installer&#x27;s real requirements.</p></section>
<section class="lf-article-section"><h2>July — Test tracking and early demand</h2><p>Run end-to-end tests from each advertising destination to the CRM. Confirm that qualified submissions are routed to the right team, replies are recorded and booking confirmations reflect actual availability. Where local demand and capacity support it, test early-booking messages with a controlled budget.</p></section>
<section class="lf-article-section"><h2>August — Develop early-booking campaigns</h2><p>Use real installation imagery and a clear consultation or quote offer. Re-engage eligible past customers and introduce the service to homeowners through Meta ads where appropriate. Monitor qualified-project fit rather than optimizing only for cheap form submissions. Do not advertise a deadline unless it is genuine.</p></section>
<section class="lf-article-section"><h2>September — Expand acquisition where justified</h2><p>Review Google Search queries for professional installation intent. Adjust location targeting and negatives, refine creative and compare the quality of quote requests. Increase spending only when lead handling and installation capacity can support it. Residential and commercial services may need different campaigns.</p></section>
<section class="lf-article-section"><h2>October — Coordinate peak-season demand</h2><p>Keep search ads, social campaigns and CRM follow-up aligned with available design visits and installation slots. Publish accurate availability messaging and prioritize inquiries using actual service fit, project scope and timing. Update landing pages when certain areas or services are fully booked.</p></section>
<section class="lf-article-section"><h2>November — Protect service quality</h2><p>In many markets this is an active installation period, but local timing varies. Focus on realistic fulfillment, fast acknowledgment and clear next steps. Reduce or redirect acquisition when capacity is limited. Track booked consultations separately from completed projects and avoid creating appointments the business cannot honor.</p></section>
<section class="lf-article-section"><h2>December — Deliver, document and prepare renewals</h2><p>Support installation and maintenance requests, capture authorized finished-display photography and make the takedown process clear. Review results as verified data becomes available. Document customer preferences and prepare appropriate renewal outreach for the following season.</p></section>

    <section class="lf-article-section">
      <h2>Campaign planning and measurement</h2>
    </section>

    <section class="lf-article-section"><h2>When should a Christmas lighting company start marketing?</h2><p>There is no single start date that works in every location. Commercial buyers and returning customers may plan months ahead; first-time homeowners may search closer to installation season. Use this calendar as a planning framework, then adjust it to local climate, demand, customer behavior and crew capacity.</p></section>
<section class="lf-article-section"><h2>How to divide marketing into four phases</h2><p>The planning phase focuses on retaining customers, improving the website and preparing creative. The early-booking phase introduces offers and tests demand. The peak-acquisition phase aligns Google Ads, Meta creative and lead qualification with availability. The fulfillment and retention phase protects customer experience and prepares the next season.</p></section>
<section class="lf-article-section"><h2>How should Google Ads change during the season?</h2><p>Before launch, validate search intent and geographic coverage. During active demand, review search terms, qualified leads and appointment outcomes. As installation capacity changes, update ad schedules, budgets and availability messages rather than continuing to pay for projects the business cannot accept.</p></section>
<section class="lf-article-section"><h2>How should Facebook and Instagram campaigns change?</h2><p>Earlier in the season, visual transformations and planning ideas can introduce the service. During the active booking period, show real installations, explain what&#x27;s included and offer a clear quote path. Test imagery, Reels and videos against qualified inquiries—not merely reach, likes or video views.</p></section>
<section class="lf-article-section"><h2>What should happen after every lead arrives?</h2><p>Capture the inquiry in the CRM with available attribution, qualify the service area and project details, assign a responsible person and offer the appropriate quote or design-visit path. Respect email and messaging permissions. Reminders must change when someone replies, books or opts out.</p></section>
<section class="lf-article-section"><h2>What metrics belong in the seasonal report?</h2><p>Review ad spending, inquiries, contact rates, qualified leads, consultations, estimates and confirmed installations separately. Where CRM attribution is trustworthy, compare outcomes by channel. Avoid reporting scheduled appointments as revenue or treating unverified platform conversions as completed jobs.</p></section>

